he other weekdays. Compare Middle Low German middewēke, English midweek. Originally meaning the middle between Sunday and Saturday, now often reinterpreted as the middle of the working week.\nThe original West Germanic word for Wednesday, *Wōdanas dag (“Odin's day”), had already been displaced in Old High German times. It is only attested in regional Middle High German as guotentac with g- for w- through dissimilation (likely reinforced by reinterpretation as “good day”). This survived until recently in Central Franconian Jodesdaach. See Wednesday for more.",{"g":"m","decl":"||/e|s/es|e|e|en|e"}]],"senses":[[6311,"mittwoch","noun","Wednesday",0,"m"]]},"murksen":{"entries":[["murksen",null,"/ˈmʊʁksn̩/","Derived from Middle Low German morken (“to crush; to mash”). Hence also abmurksen (“to murder”). Probably related with morsch (“brittle”)."]],"senses":[[389510,"murksen","verb","to botch",0,null,["colloquial","derogatory"]]]},"musischerem":{"entries":[["musischerem",null,null,null]],"senses":[[522136,"musischerem","adj","strong dative masculine/neuter singular comparative degree of musisch",0]],"inflections":[["musischerem","musisch","comparative,dative,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"nebulöse":{"entries":[["nebulöse",null,null,null]],"senses":[[524640,"nebulöse","adj","inflection of nebulös:",0],[524641,"nebulöse","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative feminine singular",1],[524642,"nebulöse","adj","strong nominative/accusative plural",2],[524643,"nebulöse","adj","weak nominative all-gender singular",3],[524644,"nebulöse","adj","weak accusative feminine/neuter singular",4]],"inflections":[["nebulöse","nebulös","feminine,nominative,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"neuwestem":{"entries":[["neuwestem",null,null,null]],"senses":[[521587,"neuwestem","adj","strong dative masculine/neuter singular superlative degree of neuw",0]],"inflections":[["neuwestem","neuw","dative,masculine,singular,strong,superlative,without-article"]]},"ohmen":{"entries":[["oh